A transport, a *netlayer*, moves a session's bytes between two peers. The core
hands each frame to whichever netlayer a
[locator]($link.page('concepts'))'s transport symbol names, and never learns which
one carried it. The transports below ship today. A peer that cannot open a socket
of its own, like a browser, reaches a socket transport through
[the relay]($link.page('relay')), or holds a direct WebRTC DataChannel to another
browser with no socket at all. If you are not sure which to reach for,
[Choosing a transport]($link.page('transports/choosing')) walks the decision.

## A transport is a resident, not a privilege

Each transport is implemented as a *resident*: the one module in `net` that
owns that transport's sockets or streams and plugs into the driver. The driver
holds a slice of residents and dispatches `connectVia` and `acceptVia` on the
transport symbol it reads off a locator. Every resident funnels through one
transport-generic `Connection` (a reader, a writer, and a close handle), so it
reuses the one framing core instead of reinventing it. A new transport is one
new resident and a registry entry; the driver and the core do not change to
admit it.

In the table, the **designator** is the peer's name inside its locator, the
part you dial ([Concepts]($link.page('concepts')) defines the vocabulary).

| Transport | Symbol | Channel | Designator | Reach for it when |
| --- | --- | --- | --- | --- |
| TCP | `tcp` | cleartext TCP, no TLS | `host:port` | a real network, or interop with Goblins over TCP |
| Keyed TCP | `keyed-tcp` | cleartext TCP, key-derived designator | Ed25519 pubkey hex; socket address in `direct-addresses` | restart-stable designators and key-squatter refusal ([ADR-0075](https://codeberg.org/jimisaacs/pancake/src/branch/main/docs/decisions/adr/ADR-0075-keyed-tcp-designator.md)) |
| In-process | `inproc` | in-memory byte pipes, no sockets | a name within one process | tests and demos that run both peers in one process |
| iroh | `iroh` | encrypted QUIC (separate artifact) | a self-authenticating node id in an `ocapn://` URI | you want an encrypted channel today, or peer-to-peer reach |
| WebRTC | `webrtc` | encrypted DataChannel over DTLS (separate artifact) | an Ed25519 designator reached through a signaling capability, not a dialable address | two browsers speaking directly, or a peer-to-peer channel a NAT would otherwise block |

### TCP

`tcp` is the default real-network transport, and the one that addresses a Goblins
peer. It is cleartext, no TLS, because `tcp` has to speak the same wire as Goblins'
cleartext `tcp`. The transport is proven against a live
[Goblins](https://spritely.institute/goblins/) peer over a real socket, then
re-exposed over the C ABI as blocking endpoint handles. Two I/O paths sit above it:
a blocking pump for cooperative request/response, and the completion loop (io_uring
on Linux, kqueue on macOS) for many connections without a thread apiece.

### Keyed TCP

`keyed-tcp` uses the same cleartext TCP sockets as `tcp`, under a different
locator grammar. The designator is
the listener's Ed25519 public key in canonical hex, and the socket address rides the
`direct-addresses` hint. The driver pins the handshake to the dialed key so a
partner can re-dial the same designator across a process restart, and a key
squatter is refused before the session opens
([ADR-0075](https://codeberg.org/jimisaacs/pancake/src/branch/main/docs/decisions/adr/ADR-0075-keyed-tcp-designator.md)).
Bindings expose `listen_keyed_tcp` / `KeyedTcpServer` and `keyed_tcp_locator`.
In Python, hosting one looks like this:

```python
server = await listen_keyed_tcp("127.0.0.1")
print(server.uri())  # the ocapn:// locator a partner dials
```

### In-process (inproc)

`inproc` opens no sockets at all. A switchboard wires a dialer and a listener through a pair of
in-memory byte pipes, one per direction, so a full OCapN conversation runs inside a
single process. It exists to prove the netlayer contract is not accidentally
socket-shaped, and it is what the examples and tests use to drive both halves of a
conversation without binding a port.

### iroh

`iroh` is the encrypted, peer-to-peer transport: QUIC carried by an
[iroh](https://www.iroh.computer/) sidecar in its own optional artifact. Its
designator is a self-authenticating node id, and that one key is at once the
network route, the OCapN designator, and the session signing key. The dedicated
[iroh page]($link.page('transports/iroh')) covers dialing by id, discovery, the
reachability postures, and scale.

### WebRTC

`webrtc` is the peer-to-peer transport for browsers and for pairs a NAT would
otherwise block: a [WebRTC](https://www.w3.org/TR/webrtc/) DataChannel,
DTLS-encrypted, in its own optional artifact. A WebRTC peer has no dialable
address; the two ends meet through a signaling capability both can reach, then
speak directly with the signaling host out of the byte path. The dedicated
[WebRTC page]($link.page('transports/webrtc')) covers the signaling exchange,
the browser arm, and the handoff.

## Encryption is the netlayer's job

Confidentiality is something a netlayer provides, not a flag the API sets. The
contract a transport meets says nothing about whether the stream is encrypted,
so each netlayer answers that for itself. `iroh` is encrypted because its QUIC
channel is keyed, and `webrtc` because its DataChannel runs DTLS. The TCP
transports stay cleartext, for the Goblins parity the TCP section names. TLS over a socket would simply
be another netlayer under the same contract, with its own transport symbol;
nothing
forbids it, and it is not written yet. Where pancake heads beyond today's
transports lives in the
[vision](https://codeberg.org/jimisaacs/pancake/src/branch/main/docs/vision.md).
